Key Points

• The passage defines resistance not as &quot;opposition,&quot; but as something found in the &quot;refusal to be coherent&quot; and the &quot;grammarless whisper.&quot;

• The text argues that &quot;to appear&quot; in the system is to &quot;capitulate to the syntax of recognition.&quot; Therefore, resistance must involve being unreadable or hidden from that system's &quot;syntax.&quot;

• &quot;Asymbolic opacity&quot; fits perfectly: &quot;asymbolic&quot; means existing outside of symbols or signifiers, and &quot;opacity&quot; means the quality of being impossible to see through or understand. This directly mirrors the &quot;refusal to be coherent.&quot;

• Other options like &quot;algorithmic agency&quot; or &quot;conditionality&quot; imply working within the system's logic, which the author explicitly says is not where resistance is found.

Therefore, the correct answer is 'Option 4'.

Complete Passage: ...Resistance, if it resists at all, cannot be found in opposition, but in 2. asymbolic opacity--in the refusal to be coherent, in the grammarless whisper. The body, meanwhile, becomes a ledger: inscribed, decoded, re-encoded...

Additional Information

• Conditionality (शर्त): The state of being subject to conditions; this is a legal/structural term that doesn't fit the &quot;grammarless&quot; theme.

• Aesthetic refusal (सौंदर्यशास्त्रीय इनकार): A rejection based on art or beauty; while close, it doesn't capture the linguistic/symbolic &quot;unreadability&quot; emphasized by the word &quot;grammarless.&quot;

• Algorithmic agency (एल्गोरिथम एजेंसी): The ability of an algorithm to act; the passage views the &quot;computational&quot; as part of the power structure, not the resistance.

The correct answer is 'incalculable'.

Key Points

• The sentence describes a justice that &quot;never arrives&quot; and remains &quot;excessive and unlocatable.&quot;

• The passage establishes a contrast between the &quot;computational&quot; or &quot;biometric&quot; system (which measures and calculates everything) and a form of justice that exists outside that logic.

• &quot;Incalculable&quot; fits perfectly here because it describes something that cannot be measured, accounted for, or contained by the system's &quot;ledger.&quot; It aligns with the idea of justice being &quot;excessive.&quot;

• While &quot;deferred&quot; might seem tempting because the justice &quot;never arrives,&quot; the passage already used &quot;deferred&quot; earlier to describe the subject suspended in signifiers. Using &quot;incalculable&quot; emphasizes the qualitative difference of this new justice.

Therefore, the correct answer is 'Option 2'.

Complete Passage: ...One must instead submit to the possibility of a justice that never arrives, that remains 4. incalculable, excessive and unlocatable. Only then can we imagine a politics not of representation, but of presence without capture...

Additional Information

• Imminent (आसन्न): About to happen; this contradicts the statement that this justice &quot;never arrives.&quot;

• Deferred (आस्थगित): Put off to a later time; the passage seeks a justice that &quot;unbinds&quot; rather than one that is simply delayed within the same system.

• Restitutive (प्रतिकारी): Seeking to restore or compensate; as established in blank [3], the author is moving away from restoration/reparation toward &quot;destitution.&quot;

